---
description: Conventional Commits: lowercase feat/fix/docs/refactor/test/chore prefix, imperative subject under 72 chars, why-first body.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Commit messages

## Subject

- Start with a conventional-commit type, lowercase:
  - `feat:` new user-facing behavior
  - `fix:` a bug fix
  - `docs:` docs and rules only
  - `refactor:` no behavior change
  - `test:`, `chore:`
- After the prefix: English imperative, total ≤ 72 characters, no trailing
  period.
- State what changed, and why when it is not obvious:
  `fix: probe zellij across homes so cargo installs can open the board`
- No issue numbers in the subject. `chore: release vX.Y.Z` is written by
  the release tool (`release.toml`) — never hand-write or hand-edit one.

## Body

- Separate subject and body with a blank line.
- Lead with the constraint, incident, or mechanism that motivates the
  change; mechanics after. Wrap prose at ~72 characters.
- Use `-` bullets for multi-part changes; leave a blank line between
  paragraphs.

## Before committing

- Run `cargo fmt` locally — CI runs `cargo fmt --check` and rejects any
  diff, including import rewraps.
